[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 803,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 607,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123842,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 607,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Metastases to the pituitary gland are tumours involving the anterior and/or posterior pituitary secondarily by spread from primary sites in other organs.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T16:30:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 804,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 608,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123843,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 608,
        "headingText": "<p>Code according to histology of metastasis</p>",
        "timestamp": "2024-11-18T12:39:55"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 805,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 609,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123844,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 609,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2E0Y &amp; XA8J35 Malignant neoplasm metastasis in other specified sites &amp; Pituitary gland</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T16:30:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 806,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 610,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123845,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 610,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T16:30:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 807,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 611,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123846,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 611,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>None</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T16:30:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 808,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 612,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123847,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 612,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The posterior pituitary is more frequently involved than the anterior gland or the pituitary capsule {24445565; 26186025}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T16:30:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 809,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 613,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123848,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 613,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Pituitary metastases usually occur in patients with generalized metastatic disease {29974330; 32189207}. Although the pituitary can be the only site involved, pituitary metastases are rarely the first manifestation {26186025; 29974330}.</p>\r\n<p>Headache, visual field defects, cranial nerve palsy, and ophthalmoplegia are presenting signs and symptoms {29974330}. Hypopituitarism and diabetes insipidus are common {29974330; 32189207}. Apoplexy is rare {29974330}. Rapid growth of a sellar tumour with infiltration of adjacent structures should raise the suspicion of metastasis. Metastases can be asymptomatic and discovered when imaging is performed for other neurological deficits {32189207}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T16:30:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 810,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 614,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123849,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 614,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Metastases represent about 0.6&ndash;1% of pituitary tumours {17287410; 26186025} and 0.14&ndash;28.1% of brain metastases at autopsy {24445565}. The incidence is increasing because of patients&rsquo; longer survival and more sensitive neuroimaging {26186025}. Peak incidence is in the sixth or seventh decade of life; there is no sex predilection {26186025; 30139817}. Metastases to pituitary neuroendocrine tumours (PitNETs)/adenomas have been reported {28383413}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T16:30:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 811,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 615,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123850,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 615,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Unknown</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T16:30:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 812,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 616,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123851,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 616,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Tumours can spread to the pituitary via direct blood supply to the posterior lobe, involve the infundibulum via the hypothalamic&ndash;hypophysial portal system, or disseminate from the suprasellar cistern {22480977; 25667485; 31645015; 32189207}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T16:30:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 819,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 623,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123852,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 623,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Metastases to the pituitary are intrasellar/suprasellar lesions, often with a dumbbell shape due to compression of the diaphragma sellae. Erosion of the sellar floor is common, and oedema of the optic apparatus can occur {26186025}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T16:30:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 813,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 617,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123853,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 617,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Any tumour can metastasize to the pituitary, with breast and lung the most common primary sites {24445565; 31645015}. Histological features are similar to those of the tumour of origin and may appear as a discrete mass, small aggregates, or single cells. Distinguishing metastatic low-grade neuroendocrine neoplasms (NENs) from a PitNET/adenoma is challenging. An absence of pituitary transcription factors favours metastasis.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T16:30:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 814,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 618,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123854,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 618,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Squash and imprint preparations show atypical cells, at times admixed with normal adenohypophysial cells.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T16:30:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 815,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 619,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123855,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 619,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Molecular testing may have a role in identifying the primary site of origin of the tumour or in guiding therapy, depending on the tumour type.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T16:30:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 818,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 622,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123856,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 622,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> deposits of non-hypophysial malignant cells in the anterior and/or posterior pituitary.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> an absence of pituitary hormones and pituitary transcription factors in tumour cells (in selected cases).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T16:30:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 816,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 620,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123857,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 620,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Union for International Cancer Control (UICC)&nbsp;/ American Joint Committee on Cancer (AJCC) staging system for the primary tumour metastatic to the pituitary gland</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T16:30:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 817,
    "bookid": 53,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 621,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 123858,
        "bookId": 53,
        "chapterId": 175,
        "headingId": 621,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The prognosis is poor {26186025}. Outcomes are improving with earlier diagnoses and better management of primary and systemic tumours {25667485; 32189207}. Prognosis and treatment response depend on the primary tumour and extent of disease. A minority of patients die from progression of the pituitary lesion {26186025}.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2024-10-09T16:30:17"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]